[
 {:name "ls", :kind "function", :text "ls(paths='.', depth=1, is_hidden=False, *, hidden=None, is_ignored=False, pattern=None, as_paths=False, limit=250, offset=0) lists directory contents from the ignore-aware walk of the host, as a compact printable STRING. `ls(dir)` gives a `path  Nd Nf` header, then the tree.\n\nThe files and closed directories of one directory share one line, separated by two spaces. Directories come first, then files, each in alphabetical order. Each open directory follows on its own line as `name/ N`, with its child count, and its children indent two spaces. A closed directory is `name/ N`, or `name/` at the depth limit. A file is `name`, or `name 120k` from 64k up. A name with whitespace is quoted.\n\nA page shows at most `limit` entries, counted level by level, so the shallow levels are complete first. `+K more (28 .md, 2 dirs)` ends a directory that continues on the next page. A capped header names the next call: `1-250 of 625 → next(r), or the same call with offset=250`. limit=None lists everything.\n\n`next(r)` gives the next page and raises StopIteration after the last, so use `next(r, None)` as the sentinel form. `r.pages()` walks the pages with a bound of 10. `r.all()` gives the rest in one unpaged page. `r.next_offset` is where the next page starts, or None.\n\n`ls([dir, ...])` gives one such section per directory, in request order, separated by a blank line. A path is a str or a pathlib.Path. A batch entry can also be a per-path spec, such as `{\"path\": dir, \"depth\": 2}`, whose options override the shared ones. Each directory pages on its own, and `r.next_offset` is then a list with one value per directory.\n\nStart at a known parent, and batch only confirmed directories. One missing, protected or non-directory path fails the batch with a host tool error. A missing path names the nearest existing directory. Read files with cat.\n\npattern=None leaves the listing unchanged. A string is a case-sensitive basename glob (*, ?, [abc], {a,b}), not a regex. It applies at every requested depth and keeps the ancestors of matches. A per-path spec can override pattern, and None disables it there. Example: `ls(dir, pattern='*snapshot*')`.\n\nDotfiles need is_hidden=True (alias: hidden=True). When hidden is not None, it overrides is_hidden. By default, the listing skips gitignored entries. is_ignored=True also lists entries that .gitignore, .ignore and the host's cache rules exclude, such as build output. A per-path spec can set it too.\n\nas_paths=True returns the same walk as a flat list of paths instead of the tree, in the same order. Each path is the requested path joined with the entry name, and directories end in '/'. A relative request gives relative paths, and an absolute or `~` request gives absolute paths. as_paths ignores limit and offset: `ls(dir, depth=3, as_paths=True)` is the whole file list, ready for cat, grep or Path().", :call "ls(paths='.', depth=1, is_hidden=False, *, hidden=None, is_ignored=False, pattern=None, as_paths=False, limit=250, offset=0)"}]
